TRINORIS multi-agents — licensing

This repository is licensed in two parts. Read this file before reusing anything.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. packages/ — Apache License 2.0
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The published implementation is open source under the Apache License, Version 2.0:

  packages/core/   @trinoris/agent-core
  packages/sdk/    @trinoris/agent-sdk
  packages/cli/    @trinoris/agent-cli

The full licence text is in each package's own LICENSE file, which is what ships to
npm. You may use, modify, distribute and sell derivative works of these packages under
Apache-2.0, including inside proprietary software. Apache-2.0 grants patent rights and
does NOT grant trademark rights: it gives you no permission to use the TRINORIS name or
the @trinoris npm scope to endorse or promote your derivative work.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2. Everything else — proprietary, all rights reserved
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Copyright (c) 2026 TRINORIS. All rights reserved.

Everything outside packages/ is proprietary and confidential and is NOT covered by
Apache-2.0. That includes, without limitation:

  specs/        the coordination protocol specification
  docs/         architecture decision records
  tests/        the verification suite
  coordination/ operational records

No licence is granted to use, copy, modify or distribute these materials.

Why the split: the implementation is a modest amount of code, while the specifications
record verified findings that were expensive to establish — which filesystem primitives
are atomic under real concurrency, which obvious alternatives silently fail under load,
and the measured failure rates that led to the current design. The code is given away so
the protocol can be adopted. The research behind it is not.

